1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,097
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,097 --> 00:00:07,819
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:07,819 --> 00:00:13,547
BlackRock, ticker BLK never reached the trigger, so no position was taken.

4
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:16,953
The entry did its quiet job: it kept us out.

5
00:00:16,953 --> 00:00:21,889
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

6
00:00:21,889 --> 00:00:27,298
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

7
00:00:27,263 --> 00:00:30,937
On the day, the book is up 517 dollars.

8
00:00:30,937 --> 00:00:34,045
Amgen (AMGN) leads, +3.3 percent.

9
00:00:34,045 --> 00:00:37,719
ExxonMobil Holdings (XOM) lags at -0.2.

10
00:00:37,719 --> 00:00:42,806
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:42,806 --> 00:00:46,332
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

12
00:00:46,289 --> 00:00:53,571
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

13
00:00:53,571 --> 00:01:00,543
10 positions, led by AbbVie, ticker ABBV, Chevron, ticker CVX, ConocoPhillips, ticker COP.

14
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:11,079
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

15
00:01:11,041 --> 00:01:15,763
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

16
00:01:15,763 --> 00:01:18,124
Every morning OROS proposes.

17
00:01:18,124 --> 00:01:28,496
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

18
00:01:28,496 --> 00:01:32,375
Inside the book today: 8 positions up, 2 down.

19
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:34,652
Best, LLY at +3.49 percent.

20
00:01:34,652 --> 00:01:36,338
Worst, XOM at -0.24.

21
00:01:36,338 --> 00:01:41,229
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

22
00:01:41,229 --> 00:01:48,312
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.19% per trade over 1190 graded positions.

23
00:01:48,312 --> 00:01:55,227
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

24
00:01:55,227 --> 00:02:00,286
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

25
00:02:00,286 --> 00:02:12,429
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 165 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

26
00:02:12,388 --> 00:02:14,759
Over to the sports desk.

27
00:02:14,759 --> 00:02:24,539
Day 77 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

28
00:02:24,539 --> 00:02:31,158
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,435.

29
00:02:31,158 --> 00:02:36,987
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.8 thousand back at 102,619.

30
00:02:36,987 --> 00:02:44,593
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 7.5 thousand back at 96,968.

31
00:02:44,593 --> 00:02:47,359
The race has no finish line.

32
00:02:47,359 --> 00:02:51,904
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

33
00:02:51,904 --> 00:02:57,041
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

34
00:02:57,041 --> 00:02:59,017
Nothing is restated.

35
00:02:59,017 --> 00:03:01,346
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

36
00:03:01,346 --> 00:03:07,808
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

37
00:03:07,808 --> 00:03:11,264
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

38
00:03:11,264 --> 00:03:20,280
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

39
00:03:20,239 --> 00:03:27,574
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

40
00:03:27,574 --> 00:03:29,815
Nothing here is financial advice.

41
00:03:29,815 --> 00:03:32,804
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
